title: A counter you can pause
mode: vhdl
chapter: Counting
check: behaves
cycles: 150
random: en=0.5
---- intro
A counter should count only when told to. With `en` (enable) at 1 it counts up; with `en` at 0 it holds its number.

Put the `+ 1` inside an `if`. When nothing gives `count` a new value, it keeps the old one.
---- task
Count up by one at each rising edge while `en` is 1, and hold the count while it is 0. `q` is the count.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity counter_en is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    en  : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end counter_en;

architecture rtl of counter_en is
    signal count : unsigned(3 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity counter_en is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    en  : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end counter_en;

architecture rtl of counter_en is
    signal count : unsigned(3 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    process (clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if en = '1' then
                count <= count + 1;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;
    q <= std_logic_vector(count);
end rtl;
---- hint1
Like the counter, with the `count <= count + 1;` inside `if en = '1' then ... end if;`.
---- hint2
Inside the clock edge: `if en = '1' then count <= count + 1; end if;`. After the process: `q <= std_logic_vector(count);`
---- solution
```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        if en = '1' then
            count <= count + 1;
        end if;
    end if;
end process;
q <= std_logic_vector(count);
```
---- done
Every timer in a computer has an enable like this, so a program can start and stop it.
